course: biology
lesson: 367
title: Animal colours: pink flamingos, rare blue animals, white albinos and black panthers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Animal colours come in two kinds: pigments, which are coloured stuff in the body like paint, and structures, tiny shapes that bend and scatter light like a soap bubble.
explain: A flamingo is pink because of paint it eats: pigment from tiny shrimps and algae. A jay's feather is blue because of shape: crush it and the blue is gone, leaving grey-brown.
explain: When the gene for a pigment changes, you get surprises: a white rabbit with pink eyes, a white tiger with dark stripes, a black leopard or a bright blue lobster.
explain: Colour is also a message: it can hide an animal in dark forest, or shout «I taste horrible» like a ladybird's red and black.
fact: Flamingo chicks hatch grey and white and turn pink only after years of eating; both parents feed them a red milk made in their throats and turn paler while they do it.
fact: Zoos add pink colouring to flamingos' food, or the birds slowly fade.
fact: Wild salmon get their pink from the same kind of pigment, eaten in tiny shrimps, and farmed salmon are fed it so the meat looks the colour people expect; without it they would be pale grey.
fact: Almost no animals make blue pigment; most blue comes from tiny structures that scatter light, and when the structure is broken the blue disappears.
fact: The blue morpho butterfly's wing scales have tiny ridges that reflect blue light, and its brown underside with eye spots makes it seem to vanish when it closes its wings.
fact: Most lobsters are dark greenish brown, and a blue one is often said to be about 1 in 2 million; all lobsters turn red when cooked because heat frees the red pigment.
fact: An albino animal cannot make melanin, the dark pigment of skin, hair and eyes, so its eyes look pink or red from the blood vessels showing through.
fact: Albino animals often see poorly, burn easily in sun and are easier for predators to spot.
fact: White tigers and white lions are not albino: a gene makes their fur pale, but they still have dark stripes or eyes, and breeding close relatives together again and again in zoos gave many white tigers health problems.
fact: A black panther is not a separate kind of animal but a leopard (in Africa and Asia) or a jaguar (in Central and South America) with extra dark pigment, and its spots still show in bright sunlight or on night camera traps.
fact: Bagheera in The Jungle Book is a black leopard.
fact: On the Malay peninsula in South-East Asia almost all leopards are reported to be black, because a black coat is good camouflage in dark, damp forest.
fact: A ladybird's spots tell you its kind, not its age: the seven-spot ladybird always has seven, and there are 2-spot, 10-spot and 22-spot kinds.
fact: A ladybird's red and black are a warning: it tastes bitter and oozes yellow liquid from its knees when grabbed.
exercise: What colour are flamingo chicks when they hatch? | Grey and white | Bright pink all over | Deep red | Black with white spots
exercise: What do flamingo parents feed their chick? | A red milk made in their throats | Pink sweets that zoo keepers give them | Only fresh water from the lake | Whole fish
exercise: What happens to zoo flamingos if their food has no pink colouring? | They slowly fade | They turn bright blue | They turn darker pink | Nothing at all happens
exercise: Where does the pink in wild salmon meat come from? | Pigment eaten in tiny shrimps | Blood running through the fish | Sunlight shining on the river water | The salmon's own bright red eggs
exercise: What would farmed salmon look like without the pigment in their food? | Pale grey | Bright green | Deep purple | Exactly the same pink
exercise: Where does most blue colour in animals come from? | Tiny structures that scatter light | Blue pigment, just like in blue paint | Blue food they eat, such as berries | Blue blood flowing just under the skin
exercise: What happens when you crush a blue jay feather? | The blue disappears and it looks grey-brown | It turns an even brighter shade of blue | It turns bright red like a cardinal | It turns green
exercise: How does a blue morpho seem to vanish when it lands? | It closes its wings and shows the brown underside | It turns invisible for a few seconds | It flies too fast for any eye to see it land | It hides its wings in a hole
exercise: What colour are most lobsters before they are cooked? | Dark greenish brown | Bright red all over | Bright sky blue | White, like the cooked meat
exercise: Why do all lobsters turn red when cooked? | Heat frees the red pigment from a protein | Cooks paint them red so they look tasty | The hot water is red and stains their shells | They blush
exercise: What can an albino animal not make? | Melanin, the dark pigment | Bones | Blood or a heartbeat | Feathers or fur of any kind
exercise: Why do albino animals often have pink or red eyes? | You see the blood vessels through the colourless eye | Their eyes are always sore and itchy | They cry red tears when they are scared or hungry | They eat red berries
exercise: Which is a problem albino animals in the wild often have? | They see poorly and are easy for predators to spot | They cannot eat any meat | They cannot have babies at all, ever, in their lives | They are too heavy to run
exercise: How can you tell a white tiger is not albino? | It still has dark stripes and dark or blue eyes | It is always smaller than an orange tiger | It has pink stripes and pink eyes instead of black | It cannot roar
exercise: What is a black panther? | A leopard or jaguar with extra dark pigment | A separate kind of black cat from Africa | A black lion | A puma whose fur was painted black by people
exercise: What kind of animal is Bagheera in The Jungle Book? | A black leopard | A black bear from the hills | A rare black lion | A huge black tiger
exercise: Why are most leopards on the Malay peninsula black? | A black coat hides them well in dark, damp forest | They roll in black mud every day to keep cool | They are a different kind of cat from other leopards | They live in caves
exercise: What do the spots on a ladybird tell you? | What kind of ladybird it is | How many years old it is now | How many babies it has had so far | How many times it has ever flown
test: «look, that flamingo chick's grey, it must be ill» What is right? | Chicks hatch grey and only turn pink after years of eating | Yes, all grey flamingos are sick and need a vet | It is a different bird, because flamingos are always born pink | It got dirty and needs a bath
test: Why would a pale adult flamingo in a zoo be a sign that something is wrong? | Its food is probably missing the pink pigment | It is getting too old to fly | It is about to turn into a swan, like the ugly duckling | It has been in the sun too long
test: Why does a peacock feather change colour as you tilt it? | Tiny structures scatter light differently at each angle | It has wet paint on it that slides around | The bird is still alive inside the feather | Your own eyes change colour as you move your head
test: The Pink Panther in cartoons is a pink big cat, but no real big cat is pink. What makes a real black panther black? | Extra dark pigment in a leopard or jaguar | Rolling in coal dust every day | Eating dark berries in the forest every night | Being an albino with no pigment
test: «black panthers have no spots» says a friend. What is true? | The spots are still there and show in bright sun or on night camera traps | Right, they are completely plain black from nose to tail | They have stripes like tigers, hidden under the black fur | They have white spots only
test: If you saw a black panther in the Amazon, what kind of big cat would it be? | A jaguar | A leopard | An African lion | A Bengal tiger
test: Count them: a boy says a ladybird with 7 spots is 7 years old. What is wrong? | Spots show the kind of ladybird, and an adult keeps the same spots all its life | Nothing is wrong, each spot is a year, so it is old for a ladybird | Ladybirds have one spot for each month | It should be 14 years, two spots per year
test: Count them: a 2-spot and a 22-spot ladybird sit on a leaf. How many spots altogether? | 24 | 20 | 4 | 44
test: Why would being bright be safer than hiding for a bitter ladybird? | Birds learn that red and black means a nasty taste | Bright colours make it fly faster than birds can chase | Birds cannot see red | Bright colours keep it warm on cold days
test: Why are albino animals more common in pets and zoos than in the wild? | In the wild they are easy to spot and see poorly, so fewer survive | Zoos make them by painting ordinary animals white | Albinos refuse to live in the wild and walk into towns | Wild albinos turn dark in the sun
test: «just caught a blue lobster mate, gonna cook it, it'll be blue on the plate» What will happen? | It will turn red, like all cooked lobsters | It will stay bright blue on the plate | It will turn green, like a cooked crab always does | It will turn white
test: Why would a black coat be less useful on the open savanna than in a dark forest? | On bright, open grassland a black cat would stand out | Black fur cannot keep out the rain on the savanna | Savanna animals cannot see the colour black | Black coats melt in the sun
exam: Identify the source of the pink colouration of the flamingo. | Pigments ingested in its diet of small crustaceans and algae | A pink pigment manufactured from sunlight within its feathers | The blood circulating beneath its feathers | A dye applied by its parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the blue colouration of most animals? | It arises from minute structures scattering light rather than from pigment | It arises from blue pigment which is abundant in the bodies of animals | It arises from the consumption of blue food | It arises from blue blood beneath the skin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the animal commonly designated a black panther. | It is a leopard or jaguar possessing an excess of dark pigment | It constitutes a separate species of black cat dwelling in Africa | It is a lion lacking a mane | It is invariably an albino animal
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an albino animal exhibits pink or red eyes. | The blood vessels are visible through the unpigmented eye | The eyes are perpetually inflamed | The animal consumes red berries and the juice colours its eyes | The eyes are coloured by red tears
